      "conclusion": "Lower average CPU and a smaller WASM module than the preceding runtime: the 1024-tree main GBDT saves more than the post-model head adds. The head adds about 6 percent of the analysis stage time and is spread over the hop by the load-leveling scheduler."
    },
    "limitations": [
      "The post-model head was trained only on inputs from MIDI 28-91. When the user range is widened beyond 28-91, out-of-range GBDT confidences are fed to the head unchanged as neighbouring inputs; head output covers MIDI 28-91 only, and other pitches keep the GBDT value.",
      "Development and held-out scores used binary16-rounded head inputs; the runtime uses binary32. The maximum probability difference is 0.0066 with 6 decision flips in about 1.29 million cells; scores were not recomputed.",
      "The held-out Slakh gain is not significant (+0.26 F1 points at the dev-frozen lag); the improvement comes mainly from real recordings.",
      "Drum-only material still produces note frames: 41 percent of Candombe and 7.7 percent of BRID frames at R160 (preceding runtime 81 and 59 percent).",
      "The live column shows the immediate output; its R160 revision replaces it 160 ms later, and a frame whose revision is missed keeps the immediate value.",
      "Synthetic low-octave tests: weak upper-octave recall and MIDI 31 lower-only precision fall below the previous thresholds with the head; accepted in favour of the held-out real-music gain.",
      "GT3 covers exact locally audited patches only; unknown patches retain their stored MIDI pitch."
    ]
